'''Lago Argentino''' is a [[lake]] in the [[Patagonia]]n [[provinces of Argentina|province]] of [[Santa Cruz Province (Argentina)|Santa Cruz]], [[Argentina]]. It is the largest freshwater lake in Argentina, with a surface area of {{convert|1415|km2|mi2|0|abbr=on}} and a maximum width of {{convert|20|mi|km|0|abbr=on}}. The lake's waters have an average depth of {{convert|155|m|ft|0|abbr=on}}, with a maximum depth of {{convert|719|m|ft|0|abbr=on}}. The deepest point of the lake is situated in the end of its narrow north-northwestern arm, in front of the retreating [[Upsala Glacier]]. This depth was discovered during a survey in 2001. The south-southwestern  arm ends in front of the [[Perito Moreno Glacier]]. The glaciers debouch into the lake at these sites, making "trenches" in the bedrock. The lake reaches {{convert|541|m|ft|abbr=on}} below mean sea level.

Lago Argentino lies within [[Los Glaciares National Park]] in a landscape accented by numerous [[glacier]]s, and the lakes of the area are fed by the glacial [[meltwater]] of several rivers. For example, the waters of [[Lake Viedma]] are fed by the [[La Leona River]], and many other smaller mountain streams. Lago Argentino's [[drainage basin]] amounts to more than {{convert|17000|km2|mi2|0|abbr=on}}. Water from Lake Argentino flows into the [[Atlantic Ocean]] through the [[Santa Cruz River (Argentina)|Santa Cruz River]].

The glaciers, the nearby town of [[El Calafate]], and the lake itself are all important tourist destinations with many hostels, hotels and other amenities catering to the outdoors located in the region. The lake in particular is appreciated for [[fishing]]. [[Perch]], [[common galaxias]] ("''puyen grande''"), [[lake trout]] and [[rainbow trout]]—in both [[Fish migration|anadromous]] and [[potamodromous]] forms—are all found.

{{lang|es|Lago Argentino}} was also the name of the [[Lago Argentino Airport|airport that served the area]] until 2000. This airport was closed and its runway incorporated into the city's road system. It has been replaced by [[Comandante Armando Tola International Airport]], which serves the town of El Calafate and Lago Argentino with many national and international flights each day.

==Flora and fauna==
Lago Argentino and its surrounding terrain is home to numerous living organisms; as of 2024, the [[biodiversity]] database [[iNaturalist]] lists nearly 500 recorded plant<ref>{{Cite web |title=Observations • iNaturalist |url=https://www.inaturalist.org/observations?place_id=14515&subview=map&taxon_id=47126&view=species |access-date=30 May 2024 |website=INaturalist.org}}</ref> and almost 400 animal<ref>{{Cite web |title=Observations • iNaturalist |url=https://www.inaturalist.org/observations?place_id=14515&subview=map&taxon_id=1&view=species |access-date=30 May 2024 |website=iNaturalist.org}}</ref> [[species]] as being found in the area, as well as nearly 100 types of [[Fungus|fungi]] and [[lichen]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Observations • iNaturalist |url=https://www.inaturalist.org/observations?place_id=14515&subview=map&taxon_id=47170&view=species |access-date=30 May 2024}}</ref>

'''Birds'''

Around 150 species of [[bird]]s have been documented in and around the lake.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Observations • iNaturalist |url=https://www.inaturalist.org/observations?place_id=14515&subview=map&taxon_id=3&view=species |access-date=30 May 2024}}</ref> Many are [[Endemism|endemic]] to the region, such as the roughly 50-60 species of [[Wader|wading birds]] and [[Anseriformes|waterfowl]]; iconic amphibious species, like [[Andean flamingo|Andean]] and [[Chilean flamingo]]s, as well as the [[black-headed duck]], [[black-necked swan]], [[cinnamon teal]], [[crested duck]], [[Coscoroba swan]], [[kelp goose]], [[silver teal]], [[torrent duck]] and many others can be found here, as well as varied species of [[coot]], [[Neotropic cormorant|cormorant]], [[Rufous-chested dotterel|dotterel]], [[Moorhen|gallinule]], [[godwit]], [[grebe]], [[gull]], [[Calidris|knot]], [[lapwing]], [[oystercatcher]], [[phalarope]], [[plover]], [[sandpiper]], [[snipe]], [[stilt]], [[tern]] and [[Tringa|yellowlegs]].

Other birds include the famous [[Andean condor]] (the largest [[New World vulture]]), [[Austral parakeet]], [[Austral pygmy owl|Austral pygmy-owl]], [[cinereous harrier]], [[crested caracara]], the flightless [[Darwin's rhea]] (the largest bird in the area), [[lesser horned owl]], [[Magellanic woodpecker]], and the [[short-eared owl]].

'''Fish'''

[[Common galaxias|''Puyen'']] (''Galaxias maculatus'') and ''[[Perch|perca]]'' (''Percichthys''), are two registered species of fish that live in the lake. Also present are introduced and non-native [[brown trout]] (''Salmo trutta''), [[chinook salmon]] (''Oncorhynchus tshawytscha''), [[lake trout]] (''Salvelinus namaycush'') and [[rainbow trout]] (''O''. ''mykiss'').<ref>{{Cite web |title=Observations • iNaturalist |url=https://www.inaturalist.org/observations?place_id=14515&subview=map&taxon_id=47178&view=species |access-date=30 May 2024 |website=iNaturalist.org}}</ref>

'''Reptiles and Amphibians'''

Of the roughly 10-15 reptile species<ref>{{Cite web |title=Observations • iNaturalist |url=https://www.inaturalist.org/observations?place_id=14515&subview=map&taxon_id=26036&view=species |access-date=30 May 2024}}</ref> recorded in the vicinity of Lago Argentino, the majority of them are [[lizard]]s ([[Squamata]]) of the genera ''[[Diplolaemus]]'' and ''[[Liolaemus]]'', including several species of tree "iguanas", smooth-headed and smooth-throated lizards.

[[Frog|Anurans]] ([[frog]]s and [[toad]]s) that inhabit the lake include ''[[Alsodes|Alsodes coppingeri]]'', the [[Nannophryne variegata|Eden Harbour toad]] (''Nannophryne variegata''), the [[Pleurodema bufoninum|large four-eyed frog]] (''Pleurodema bufoninum''), [[Batrachyla nibaldoi|Nibaldo's wood frog]] (''Batrachyla nibaldoi''), and the [[Chaltenobatrachus|Puerto Eden frog]] (''Chaltenobatrachus grandisonae'').<ref>{{Cite web |title=Observations • iNaturalist |url=https://www.inaturalist.org/observations?place_id=14515&subview=map&taxon_id=20978&view=species |access-date=30 May 2024}}</ref>

==Climate==
Argentino Lake has a [[cold desert climate]] (Köppen ''BWk''). 
